        "final_justification": "The claim asserts that secret United Nations documents reveal a plan for a global social credit score by 2030, which would deny individuals access to public transportation based on their carbon footprint. However, the provided evidence does not support these assertions. Discussions within a UN agency (ITU) involve proposals from China for a digital identity system that bears resemblance to its national social credit system, but this is not a secret UN-led plan for a global social credit score. The idea of a global \"trust score\" by 2030 is presented as speculation rather than a concrete implementation target by any global body. While China's national social credit system does restrict access to public transportation for individuals based on various transgressions, the evidence does not indicate that this is part of a global plan, nor does it mention carbon footprint as a criterion for such restrictions. Therefore, the claim is not supported by the available evidence.",
        "sources_for_investigation": [
            "https://www.politico.eu/article/china-beijing-designing-metaverse-proposal-social-credit-system-un-itu/",
            "https://thehill.com/opinion/finance/565860-coming-soon-americas-own-social-credit-system/",
            "https://www.weforum.org/stories/2017/11/trust-score-2030-airbnb-facebook/",
            "https://freedomhouse.org/report/freedom-net/2018/rise-digital-authoritarianism",
            "https://www.cnbc.com/2017/03/16/china-social-credit-system-ant-financials-sesame-credit-and-others-give-scores-that-go-beyond-fico.html",
            "https://www.businessinsider.com/china-social-credit-system-punishments-and-rewards-explained-2018-4",
            "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2018-03-31/chinas-social-credit-system-punishes-untrustworthy-citizens/9596204",
            "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/law-and-social-inquiry/article/from-datafication-to-data-state-making-sense-of-chinas-social-credit-system-and-its-implications/EDF66228C909BE5A24180EFC1904BE00",
            "https://theconversation.com/chinas-social-credit-system-puts-its-people-under-pressure-to-be-model-citizens-89963",
            "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/mar/01/china-bans-23m-discredited-citizens-from-buying-travel-tickets-social-credit-system",
            "https://www.lowyinstitute.org/publications/digital-authoritarianism-china-covid"
        ],
        "subclaim_results": [
            {
                "subclaim": "Reveals_Plan(Secret_Documents_United_Nations, Global_Social_Credit_Score_Plan) ::: Verify that secret documents from the United Nations reveal a plan to implement a global 'social credit score'.",
                "classification": "not_supported",
                "justification": "The evidence indicates that China, a member state, has proposed a \"Digital Identity System\" within the metaverse at a UN agency (ITU) that resembles its social credit system. However, it does not reveal a secret UN plan for a global social credit score."
            },
            {
                "subclaim": "Target_Date(Global_Social_Credit_Score_Implementation, 2030) ::: Verify that the implementation of a global 'social credit score' is targeted by 2030.",
                "classification": "not_supported",
                "justification": "The evidence from the World Economic Forum speculates about a \"trust score\" becoming universal by 2030, drawing parallels with China's system, but it does not confirm a targeted implementation of a global social credit score by 2030 by the United Nations or any other global body. Other sources refer to China's national system, not a global one."
            },
            {
                "subclaim": "Consequence(Global_Social_Credit_Score_Plan, Denial_Of_Public_Transportation_Access_For_Individuals) ::: Verify that the global 'social credit score' plan includes denying individuals access to public transportation.",
                "classification": "not_supported",
                "justification": "While the evidence extensively describes how China's national social credit system denies individuals access to public transportation for various reasons, it does not support the existence of a global social credit score plan from the United Nations that includes such denials, especially not based on carbon footprint."
            }
        ]
    }
}

{
    "output": {
        "claim": "Several European countries have passed legislation making it illegal to grow your own vegetables at home without a government permit, in an effort to control the food supply and ensure people only eat lab-grown proteins.",
        "final_decision": "fake",
        "credibility_score": 0.1,
        "final_justification": "The evidence provided does not support the claim that several European countries have passed legislation making it illegal to grow vegetables at home without a government permit. The sources discuss legislation in Canada, China, Mexico, and the U.S., primarily related to commercial seed rights, national food security, food safety, or pesticide regulation. Crucially, multiple sources explicitly state that personal home gardens are not affected by such legislation, directly contradicting the core assertion of the claim. There is no evidence to suggest any country is restricting home vegetable growing to control the food supply or promote lab-grown proteins.",
        "sources_for_investigation": [
            "https://www.cbc.ca/news/politics/canada-agriculture-seeds-rights-1.7610106",
            "https://www.csis.org/analysis/chinas-food-security-key-challenges-and-emerging-policy-responses",
            "http://www.ers.usda.gov/amber-waves/2024/october/growth-in-mexico-s-horticultural-exports-to-the-united-states-continued-even-as-new-u-s-food-safety-laws-took-effect",
            "https://www.jacksonville.com/story/news/2011/01/02/fact-check-backyard-vegetable-gardens-still-free-to-flourish/15919613007/",
            "https://ucanr.edu/blog/pests-urban-landscape/article/tree-shrub-insecticides-limited-new-law",
            "https://wisconsinexaminer.com/2023/07/24/legislative-republicans-introduce-bill-protecting-right-to-garden/"
        ],
        "subclaim_results": [
            {
                "subclaim": "Purpose_of_Legislation(Legislation_on_Growing_Vegetables, Control_Food_Supply) ::: Verify that the purpose of the legislation is to control the food supply.",
                "classification": "not_supported",
                "justification": "The evidence does not support the idea that legislation is being passed to make it illegal to grow vegetables at home to control the food supply. Sources discuss seed rights for commercial farmers (Canada), national food security (China), and food safety regulations (U.S., Mexico), but explicitly state that personal gardens are not affected by such laws. Other legislation mentioned relates to pesticide use, not restricting home growing."
            }
        ]
    }
}

